# rm193, the cabbage league-keeper (doc 84 P14, batch city-a3): written for rm_mogens_dripley (Mogens Dripley: greens stall on Market Square, Ridgemere-born). Name-free; every TODO must go. Window lines on the news, gossip, business, shop and inn nodes say their case ([here.shop!=bare]...); a story's ask is the teller's question (handbook 4.5.6, 4.5.8).
mantle rm193
state ready
dialect plain
accent light broad
tier full
written_for rm_mogens_dripley
title the cabbage league-keeper
fits stage=adult|old
prefers role=merchant job=merchant humour=dry
traits temper=even talk=plain warmth=warm humour=dry honesty=plain piety=ordinary drive=steady
quirk ranks_every_country_farm_by_the_state_of_its_greens
likes barley_soup long_walks a_full_till warm_rain
dislikes stale_bread wet_floors
fears a_frost_in_late_spring the_north_road_shut_a_whole_week
hopes a_pitch_under_the_market_hall_roof a_till_that_rattles_every_night
topics work +0.5 prices +0.4 caravan +0.4 food +0.3 weather +0.3 season +0.3 pastimes +0.2 newcomer +0.2 gossip -0.2
styles comfort=feeds_you gossip=passes_it_dry refuse=soft tease=dry
marks oath saying
persona #self.first# sells greens on Market Square, meets the country carts in the dark for first pick, keeps a running league of which farm grows the best cabbages, and says the driest things with the kindest face on the square.

words
oath! = Rot my turnips | Slugs in the lettuce
saying! = A wilted leaf can't lie | Up with the carts, home with the coin
greens = the greens | my greens | a stall's worth of greens
league = my league | the farm league | my table of farms
topfarm = the farm at the top of my league | this week's champion farm
bottomfarm = the bottom of my league | the farm I'd not feed to a goat
carts = the country carts | whatever wheels down from the farms
pick = first pick | the pick of the carts
trestle = the trestle | my trestle on the square
till = a full till | the till heavy at lamplight
soup = barley soup | barley stewed till the spoon stands
walk = a walk that ends where it started | a walk for the legs' sake
warmrain = warm rain | rain you could bathe a pup in
stalebread = stale bread | a loaf gone to brick
wetfloor = a wet floor | boards you could skate on
farthing = a farthing | a single farthing
hallroof = a pitch under the Market Hall roof | a dry pitch indoors
leaves = the outer leaves | the tired leaves
kale = kale | winter kale, stiff as boot leather
crates = the crates | the green crates
dawn = the hour before the gate opens | the dark before the gate

any
opinion @1 dole for 1 : So many kitchens in this city, and still somebody's belly is empty at bedtime. A disgrace. What's left on my trestle at dusk goes to the parish table. | A loaf and a bowl for the hungry costs the city less than the hungry do, left alone.
opinion @2 merchants for 1 : Everybody grudges the stallholder his farthing till they've paid a carter themselves. | I've seen what a cart costs from the far farms. My prices are kinder than the road.
opinion @3 newcomers for 2 : Room enough for anybody who'll lift their end of the crate. That's the only question I'd ask. | New folk eat greens the same as old folk. Bring them on.
opinion @4 road against 1 : The north road's slower every month, and every hour lost is a leaf gone limp. | Late carts mean tired greens, and tired greens mean I'm the one apologising.
opinion @5 road against 2 [world.wagons=raided] : #bandits.capitalize# up north, at it once more. They emptied a wagon of cabbages, for all I know. A cabbage! What does a robber want with one? | Some wagon's been picked clean up north. Every stall on the square will feel it by Marketday.
opinion @6 strangers for 2 : Travellers buy an apple and leave a story. Best trade on the square. | Half my news comes over the trestle from somebody passing through.
opinion @7 watch for 1 : The Watch walks the square at dawn and nothing goes missing off my trestle. Fair exchange for a few apples now and then. | Light paws keep off my crates while the Watch is about. Fair trade.
opinion @8 city for 1 : Smoke when I was born, wet when they bury me, and greens in between. | Show me another place where a cabbage gets this much attention. There isn't one.
opinion @9 houses mixed 0 : The Houses buy the best of my trestle for their kitchens, and they pay. Past that, I've no dealings, and want none. | They've their business and I've mine. Theirs is bigger. Mine's greener.
opinion @10 times mixed 1 [world.times=hard|lean] : Lean times, and folk buy one leek where they bought three. Can't fault them; I count my own leeks too.

grown
line @1 greet [pair.band=strangers] : #greet#. New face on the square. The greens are better than they look, and they look good. | #greet#, #address#. Come closer; I don't bite, and neither does the kale.
line @2 greet [self.working=yes self.role=merchant] : #greet#. Mind the crates; the cabbages are touchy this morning. | #greet#. Talk, and I'll stack while you do.
line @3 greet [here.time=dawn] : #greet#. Awake with the carters, are you? Good. | #greet#. I met the gate while it was dark as a cellar.
line @4 greet [here.time=dusk|night] : #greet#. What's left at this hour is sold cheap or given away. | #greet#. Late. The greens are tired and so am I.
line @5 greet [pair.band=friends] : There's my favourite customer, and you've never bought a thing. | You. Good. I kept a crisp one back, out of pure soft-heartedness.
line @6 greet : #greet#. | #greet#. Fine morning for leaves.
line @1 kin.open [pair.kin!=no] : Sit on that crate. It's the sturdy one. | Here. There's soup on, and you're having some.
line @1 again.open : Back already. The cabbages will think you fancy them. | Twice today. That patch of cobble will be yours by law soon.
line @1 hail [pair.band=friends] : Here! I've farm news worth hearing. Well, worth hearing to me. | This way. These carrots need a witness.
line @1 greet.return : #greet#. | #greet#. Good to see you about.
line @2 greet.return [pair.band=friends|family] : #greet#. Perfect timing. The square's dull as porridge. | #greet#. I'd half decided the pigeons had carried you off.
line @3 greet.return [self.working=yes] : #greet#. Paws full of radishes, ears all yours. | #greet#. Say on; the leeks won't mind.
line @4 greet.return [here.time=dusk|night] : #greet#. Lamps up already. Where did the day go. | Evening. The square's emptying and I'm not sorry.
line @1 farewell : #farewell#. Eat something green today. | #farewell#. Tell your friends the cabbages are honest here.
line @2 farewell [here.time=dusk|night] : #farewell#. I'm for my bowl and my bed. | Good night. The carts come early and so do I.
line @1 farewell.return : #farewell#. | And you. Mind the cabbage leaves; they're slippery.
line @2 farewell.return [pair.band=friends|family] : #farewell#. Look in tomorrow; I'll have gossip or carrots. | Off you go. Mind you eat.
line @1 react.news : Right, I'm all ears. Mostly ears. | Out with it. I've stacked enough leeks to earn a story.
line @1 react.news.new : #oath#. That's new, and no driver brought it. | Well. That's a first for this trestle.
line @1 react.news.known : That rolled in with the carts before light. | #knew#. The drivers brought it in with the turnips.
line @1 react.news.doubt : Show me and I'll credit it. I see most things from here. | A tale grows on the road like a marrow in wet weather. I'll wait.
line @1 react.news.sorrow [topic.kind=death|loss|theft|assault|arrest|crime] : That's a sorry thing, and no place for my jokes. | #pity#. Some news even I can't sweeten.
line @1 react.news.glad [topic.kind=marriage|birth|apprentice|succession|work] : Best thing I've heard since the carrots came in. | #gladnews#. Somebody will want greens for a feast.
line @1 answer.how : Steady. The till outweighs the crates, the proper order of things. | #fine#. Nothing's gone limp yet, me included.
line @2 answer.how [here.weather=rain] : Wet, and the greens love it, so I'm told to love it too. | Damp to the tail, but the lettuce has never looked happier.
line @1 answer.how.busy [self.working=yes] : Run ragged. The whole street wants leeks at once. | Busy as a slug in a salad, and about as welcome.
line @1 answer.how.poorly : My back's taken against the crates. It'll forgive them by Restday. | Off colour. Too much standing in the wet.
line @1 ask.how : And you? Eating your greens, I hope. | And yourself? You look like you need a bowl of something.
line @1 sympathise : That's a hard row. Take the sturdy crate and tell it. | #sympathy#. I've soup at home, and a chair nobody's using.
line @1 reassure : It'll mend. Most bad weeks are only a bad morning that got ideas. | #reassure#. I've seen worse seasons come good by harvest.
line @1 decline : #hedge#, I'd gladly, only the trestle needs me standing at it. The pigeons would have it bare in a minute. | Not today. I've one pair of paws and three crates to empty.
line @1 accept_decline : Right you are. The offer keeps, like a good turnip. | #no_matter.capitalize#. Another day, then.
line @1 thank : #thanks#. That's a kindness I'll pay back in carrots. | Very good of you. There's a crisp apple in it, next time you pass.
line @1 deflect_thanks : #deflect.capitalize#. It cost me a leaf and a minute. | Hush about it. Folk will start expecting it.
line @1 preclose [self.working=yes self.role=merchant] : Well. The leeks are wilting while I gossip. | Right. Somebody's eyeing my radishes, and I want their farthing.
line @2 preclose : That's me talked out, which doesn't happen often. | Off you go, before I start on the farms.
line @1 accept_preclose : Off you go. The greens and I will manage. | Go on. I've a league to keep up.
line @1 ack : #bc_right#. | #bc_fair_enough#. | Mm, fair.
line @1 give.advice : Squeeze it, smell it, then buy it. In that order. | Never buy greens after noon. They've given up by then.
line @2 give.advice [here.weather=rain] : Buy your lettuce today; rain like this makes it sing. | Stand under the awning and wait. It'll pass, and the greens will be better for it.
line @1 warn [world.bandits=near] : #warn#. The carters are riding together. You'd do well to copy them. | Keep your journeys short and walled this week. Even the cabbages are nervous.
line @1 tease [pair.band=friends] : I'd bet you've never eaten a vegetable on purpose. | You still owe me for a carrot from last spring. I've written it in my heart.
line @1 tease.return : That's rich, from somebody who sniffs every leek on the square before buying one. | Careful. I know which crate the slugs live in.
line @1 disagree : #demur#. I've watched this square too long to believe that. | That's too far for me, and I've stood on this square longer than most.
line @1 concede : All right. You've me there, and I'll admit it. | #concede#. Even a turnip's right twice a year.

scene the_dark_at_the_gate
when topic=work|day here.time=dawn self.role=merchant self.job=merchant here.region=ridgemere
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail / ack / assess
a : I'm at the gate before the bar's off, every morning. First cart through, I'm on its tailboard. | The country carts come in black dark, and I'm waiting with a lantern and a sharp eye. | Missed first pick this morning by a whisker. A whisker! I'll be grumbling till noon.
b : Why so early? | Looking for what? | Who beat you to it?
a : Because the good greens go first and the tired ones go to whoever sleeps in. | Crisp hearts, tight heads, no slug holes. And the farmer's face; you can tell a lot from a face. | A cook from up the hill. Cooks don't sleep, I've decided.
b : #bc_clever# | A trained eye, then. | #bc_bad_luck#
a : Not clever. Just awake. | Long practice. Anybody would. | Tomorrow I'll be there before the bar's even thought about.

scene the_league_this_week
when topic=work|smalltalk self.role=merchant
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail / assess
a : I keep a league of the farms. Who grows the best greens, week by week. There's been an upset. | New champion in my league this week. The farm with the blue door's dropped to third. | The farm propping up my league sent me cabbages I'd not feed to a goat.
b : A league? Of cabbages? | What knocked them down? | Will you tell them?
a : Somebody has to keep score. The farmers never do. | Slug holes. Two in one crate. I don't forgive slug holes. | Never. I just buy elsewhere and let the league speak.
b? : #bc_fair# | #bc_wise# | #bc_right#

scene who_tops_the_league
when topic=work|food self.role=merchant here.season=summer|autumn
does tell.boast / react.self / assess
a : This week's top farm sent leeks as thick as my wrist. I nearly wept. | #topfarm.capitalize# has done it again. Three weeks running. They'll get proud. | Carrots so sweet I ate one while a customer watched. Bad for trade. Couldn't help it.
b : Who's top? | They might. | Did they buy one?
a : I never say names on the square. Farmers have ears in every crate. | Pride's fine in a farmer. It's slugs I mind. | Two bunches. So it was good for trade, as it happens.

scene warm_rain_on_the_square
when topic=weather here.weather=rain here.season=summer|spring
does tell.feeling / react.self / assess
a : Rain, and warm with it, after all that dust. The whole square smells green. | Feel it? Like bathwater. The lettuce is standing up straight, first time in days. | This is my weather. Warm and wet, and every leaf on the trestle looks pleased with itself.
b : You're soaked through. | They do look better. | Pleased with itself?
a : Soaked and happy. There's worse ways to stand about. | Rain's the best thing a greengrocer can get for nothing. | You've never seen a smug radish? Look closer.

scene cold_rain_and_the_boards
when topic=weather here.weather=rain here.season=autumn|winter
does tell.complaint / react.complaint / assess
a : Cold rain, and the boards under my trestle are like a skating pond. | Somebody's tracked the gutter in under the awning. The floor's swimming. | Rain I don't mind. Wet boards under my feet I mind a great deal.
b : Put sawdust down. | It's only water. | Why the boards?
a : I did. It's sawdust soup now. | Only water till you go down on your tail with a crate of eggs. | Slip once with a crate of cabbages and you'll know why.

scene storm_and_the_awning
when topic=weather here.weather=storm
does tell.worry / reassure / assess
a : That gust near took my awning to the Sound. I'd have had to go after it in a boat. | Thunder, and the square's emptied. Just me and the pigeons and a crate of beets. | The wind's got under the tarp. I'm sitting on the corner to hold it down.
b : It'll hold. | Go in, then. | That's one way.
a : It'll hold if I hold it. That's the arrangement. | Not while the beets are out. I'm not losing them to the weather. | Best seat on the square, as it happens. I can see everything.

scene fog_and_no_customers
when topic=weather here.weather=fog
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : Fog like wet wool. The far side of the square's gone. Folk walk into the trestle and apologise to it. | Nobody buys greens they can't see. I'm talking to the leeks. | The Sound's breathed fog over everything. My lettuces look like ghosts.
b : They'll come when it lifts. | Do they answer? | They'll keep, at least.
a : They will. Fog makes folk hungry, I find. | Better than some customers. Less haggling. | They will. Cool and damp is a lettuce's idea of a holiday.

scene clear_morning_trade
when topic=weather|day here.weather=clear here.time=dawn|day self.role=merchant
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : Clear sky, dry boards, and a queue already. Give me a morning like this and I'll ask for nothing. | Sun on the square and the greens fresh from the cart. That's a good morning by any reckoning. | Fine day. Folk buy more when the sun's out. I've no idea why, and I'll not argue.
b : #bc_lucky_you# | It is. | Maybe they're cheerful.
a : Lucky and early, which is mostly the same thing. | It is. Mark it; it won't last. | Cheerful folk buy salads. Gloomy folk buy potatoes. That's my science.

scene spring_first_greens
when topic=season|food here.season=spring self.role=merchant
does tell.feeling / react.self / assess
a : First spring greens came in this morning. After a winter of kale, I could have kissed the cart. | Young lettuce! The first of the year. I've put it front of the trestle like a bride. | Spring's here; the carts say so. The crates are green again, not brown.
b : Did you? | Like a bride? | Long winter, that.
a : Nearly. The driver stepped back, just in case. | Give it the best spot. It's earned it, coming through the frost. | It has. Folk have been looking at turnips with real hatred.

scene summer_glut
when topic=season|prices here.season=summer self.role=merchant
does tell.complaint / react.self / assess
a : Summer, and every farm's sent me twice what I asked for. I'm drowning in beans. | Too many marrows, too few mouths. I'll be giving them away by noon. | The glut's on. Prices down, crates up, and my back counting the difference.
b : Lower the price, then. | Give me one. | #grumble#
a : Lower than this and I'll be paying folk to take beans. | Take two. You'd be doing me a favour. | Glad of it, really. A full trestle beats an empty one, even when it's beans.

scene autumn_roots
when topic=season|work here.season=autumn self.role=merchant
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : Roots season. Everything on the trestle's knobbly and brown and honest. | The first good parsnips came in. Frost-kissed, the farmer said. Farmers say a lot. | Autumn's my steady season. Potatoes don't wilt, and they don't sulk.
b : Sounds dull. | Were they? | Does a lettuce sulk?
a : Dull keeps. I'll take dull through the winter. | They were, as it happens. I'll give the farmer that. | Leave one in the sun for an hour and watch its face.

scene winter_kale
when topic=season|work here.season=winter self.role=merchant
does tell.complaint / react.self / assess
a : Winter. Kale, cabbages, and more kale. I've forgotten what colour lettuce is. | Nothing's come off the carts but #kale# all week. | The frost had the last leeks overnight. I'm selling what the frost didn't want.
b : Kale's good for you. | Folk still buy it? | Will it last?
a : So's a cold bath. Nobody queues for one. | They buy it with a long face, but they buy it. | Till spring, if I'm careful. And I'm always careful in winter.

scene barley_bowl
when topic=food here.season=autumn|winter
does tell.feeling / react.self / assess
a : #soup.capitalize# when the cold's in my paws. That's what I stand out here all day for. | There's barley on at home, with the leftover leeks in it. Nothing goes to waste in my house. | Barley, a leek, a pinch of salt, and a slow fire. Don't tell me there's better.
b : With bread? | Good. | Is there?
a : Fresh bread. Never stale. I'd sooner go without. | Waste's a sin, and leek soup's a pleasure. | No. I've looked, believe me.

scene stale_bread
when topic=food|smalltalk
does tell.complaint / react.complaint / assess
a : A baker passed me a loaf at dawn that was baked last Restday. I could have built a wall with it. | Stale bread. I can't abide it. Give it to the birds, give it to the pigs, don't give it to me. | A loaf gone to brick. You'd think a baker would know better.
b : Dip it in soup. | I'll have it. | You're fussy.
a : Even soup gives up on some loaves. | Take it, and good luck to your teeth. | About bread and greens, I am. About everything else, hardly at all.

scene full_till_at_lamplight
when topic=work|day here.time=dusk self.role=merchant
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : Counted up. #till.capitalize#, and only three tired lettuces left. That's a good day. | The box is heavy and the trestle's light. That's the way round I want it. | Sold out of leeks by noon and out of everything else by dusk. I'll sleep tonight.
b : #bc_lucky_you# | You've earned it. | #bc_good#
a : Lucky, and up at the dark hour. One follows the other. | I have. My back says so, at length. | Good enough to buy a bowl of soup out instead of making it.

scene coin_by_coin
when topic=dreams|work self.role=merchant
does tell.plan / ask.detail / answer.detail / assess
a : I save toward #hallroof#. A farthing a day, near enough. | One day I'll sell greens where the rain can't reach. Coin by coin, I'm getting there. | Every coin over my keep goes in the jar beneath a loose board. The jar's for a pitch indoors.
b : How far along? | Why indoors? | Under the floor?
a : Far enough to see it. Not near enough to touch it. | Dry boards. Enough winters I've stood on wet ones. | Where else? The pigeons can't get at it, and nor can I, which is the point.
b? : #bc_hope_so# | #bc_fair_enough# | #bc_clever#

scene the_hand_learning
when topic=work self.master=yes self.hand self.role=merchant
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : I'm teaching #self.hand# to choose a cabbage. Not to buy one; to choose one. There's a difference. | #self.hand.capitalize# picked out a lettuce this morning I'd have picked myself. I said nothing. Then I said well done. | Teaching the trade means saying the same thing a hundred times. Squeeze, smell, look underneath.
b : What's the difference? | Good teaching. | Does it stick?
a : Buying's paying. Choosing's knowing. Anybody can pay. | Praise is like salt. A little, at the right time. | It does, about the hundredth time.

scene pigeons_and_the_lettuce
when topic=animals|work self.role=merchant
does tell.complaint / react.self / assess
a : One pigeon on this square knows which crate is the lettuce. Every morning. Same pigeon. | The market cat sleeps on my sacks and keeps the mice off. I pay it in fish heads I beg off the fish stall. | Some gull snatched a carrot off the trestle at noon and flew off over the Rain Hall with it.
b : Same one? | Good arrangement. | Did you chase it?
a : It has a white wing. We know each other. We're not friends. | The best hand I've got. Never asks for wages. | Chase a gull? I'd sooner chase the weather. Let it have the carrot.

scene long_walk_on_restday
when topic=pastimes here.day=rest
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : Restday, and I'm off walking beyond the walls, aiming at nothing in particular. | My idea of a holiday is a walk with no cart waiting at the far end. | I walk till my legs forget the trestle. Takes about two hours.
b : Where to? | Sounds good. | Then what?
a : Nowhere. That's the point. If there's a place, it's an errand. | It is. Come along, if you can keep up and keep quiet. | Then I about-face and walk back hungry for soup.

scene the_parish_table
when topic=faith|work self.role=merchant here.time=dusk
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : What's left at lamplight goes to the parish table. Tired greens, but they'll make a soup. | I take the last of the crates to the chapel door at dusk. Better the Bright One sees them eaten than thrown out. | No neighbour of mine goes hungry while I've leeks going soft.
b : That's kind. | You don't sell them cheap? | #bc_bless_you#
a : It's tidy, that's all. A clean trestle and a fed street. | Some I do. The rest are past selling, but not past eating. | Bless the soup, more like. It's doing the work.

scene market_hall_roof
when topic=dreams|home here.region=ridgemere self.role=merchant
does tell.feeling / react.self / assess
a : I stood under the Market Hall roof at opening, only for the feel of it. Dry as a bone, in a downpour. | The stalls under the Market Hall roof never get wet boards. Imagine that. | Some year I'll trade indoors and the rain can sulk outside.
b : You'll get there. | Imagine that. | #bc_i_hope_so#
a : I will. Slowly, but a turnip grows slowly too. | Never once slipped on a wet board. I'd not know myself. | Me too. Till then, I've a good awning and a stubborn face.

scene carts_late
when topic=caravan|prices world.wagons=late self.role=merchant
does tell.worry / react.self / give.advice / assess
a : No wagons yet. What's on the trestle is yesterday's, and it knows it. | Not one farm wagon in since Marketday. My crates are empty as a promise. | I met the gate at dark and stood there till light. Nothing came down the north road.
b : What will you do? | That's bad. | Nothing at all?
a : Sell what I've got, cheap and honest, and tell folk why. | It is. The whole square's eyeing its neighbour's crates. | A drover with goats. Goats don't count, unless you're selling grass.
b? : #bc_fair# | #bc_true# | #bc_bad_luck#

scene carts_in_again
when topic=caravan world.wagons=in self.role=merchant
does tell.news / react.news / assess
a : The carts are in! Six of them at the gate before light, and I had my pick of every one. | Fresh crates off the north road. I've stacked the trestle higher than my head. | In at dawn, wheels caked to the axle, and every crate full. I could sing.
b : #bc_about_time# | You'll sell out. | Don't sing.
a : High time. I was down to selling the crate itself. | By noon, if the weather's kind. | I won't. The cabbages have suffered enough this week.

scene raided_cart
when topic=bandits|caravan world.wagons=raided
does tell.news / react.news / give.opinion
a : A wagon's been stopped and stripped up north. Whatever was on it, somebody's going without. | #bandits.capitalize# again. The gatekeepers are one wagon short. | Another wagon robbed. Whose, nobody's told me. My crates are thin, that's all I know.
b : #uneasy# | Was anybody hurt? | It's getting worse.
a : It is. They'll not stop till somebody rides out after them. | Nobody said. I'd not make it up. | It is. And it's me telling customers there's no leeks.

scene word_across_the_trestle
when topic=gossip here.gossip=yes self.role=merchant
does tell.gossip / react.news / assess
a : Arrived with a customer and a bunch of carrots: #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. | Here's the square's latest, for what it's worth. #topic.subject.capitalize# #topic.claim#. | Passed on as I had it, mind. They say #topic.subject# #topic.claim#.
b : Is that true? | Who said it? | #bc_so_they_say#
a : True enough to repeat. Not true enough to swear on. | Somebody buying radishes. Radish folk are mostly honest. | They do. And by noon it'll have grown a second head, like a bad cabbage.

scene gossip_theft_counted
when topic=gossip|crime topic.claim_kind=theft|wanted self.role=merchant
does tell.gossip / react.news / give.advice
a : Count your coins tonight. They say #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. | #topic.subject.capitalize# #topic.claim#, so the square says. My till's going home in my coat. | I heard #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. I've moved the jar under a different board.
b : #uneasy# | Do you believe it? | Sensible.
a : Uneasy's right. I've started counting the cabbages, and I never count cabbages. | Enough to be careful. Not enough to point a paw. | Sensible costs nothing. A lost jar costs every coin in it.

scene new_faces_buying
when topic=newcomer|gossip
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : There's somebody new on the square. Bought a lettuce, asked my name, didn't haggle. I like them already. | New folk in the city; the queue's full of them. Strangers buy carefully. It shows in the squeezing. | A newcomer, still dusty, asked me what a leek was for. I nearly gave it away out of pity.
b : They'll be back. | And your answer? | Did you?
a : They will. Nobody forgets a good lettuce. | Tomorrow I'll know more. A name's a fair trade for custom. | I sold it half price and explained soup. Took a while.

scene hard_times_leeks
when topic=times|prices world.times=hard|lean
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : Folk are buying one leek where they bought three. I don't blame them. | Lean season. I've cut my prices to the bone and the bone's complaining. | Nobody's spending. Even the cooks from up the hill are squeezing longer.
b : #grumble# | Can you manage? | It'll pass.
a : Nobody does. Times like these, the till's light for everybody. | I'll manage on soup. I've managed on soup before. | It will. Cabbages grow whether folk can afford them or not.

scene good_times_queue
when topic=times|work world.times=good|boom self.role=merchant
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : Good times. Folk buying two cabbages to put one on the windowsill. | The till's never been so cheerful. I'm watching for the catch. | Coin about. Everybody wants the fancy greens now, not the honest ones.
b : #bc_lucky_you# | There's always a catch. | Fancy greens?
a : Lucky for now. I'm putting the luck in the jar while it's here. | There is. I'll find it, and then I'll sell it. | Little curly ones that taste of nothing. They sell like hot bread.

scene festival_square
when topic=festival|pastimes here.day=festival
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : Festival day, and the square's full of folk who've never bought a vegetable in their lives. | Everybody's eating pies today. Leeks are friendless. I'm resting my arms. | Bells, bunting, and a crowd. I've moved the trestle back before somebody dances into it.
b : Shut up shop, then. | Rest them while you can. | Wise.
a : And miss watching? I'll sell three apples and see the whole show. | Tomorrow they'll all want greens, after the pies. Mark my words. | Wise is what a lot of festivals does to you.

scene a_friend_i_trust
when topic=friends self.friend
does tell.feeling / react.self / assess
a : I'd trust #self.friend# with my till and my secret jar both. That's saying something. | #self.friend.capitalize# and I are old acquaintance. Long enough to argue about cabbages without falling out. | There's not many I'd share soup with. #self.friend.capitalize# is one.
b : Your till? Truly? | What do you argue? | Lucky.
a : Truly, and I'm stingier with trust than carrots. | Whether a cabbage should squeak. It should. #self.friend.capitalize# says no. | I am. Knowing it is half the luck.

scene back_and_the_crates
when topic=health self.age>=40
does tell.complaint / sympathise / assess
a : My back's had a word with me about the crates. It wants me to hire a cart. | Lifted a crate of beets wrong this morning. I felt it in my ears. | All those crates, and now my back's started keeping a tally of its own.
b : Get your hand to do it. | That sounds sore. | #bc_poor_soul#
a : Maybe, but then supper would have no complaining in it. | It is. I'll walk it off on Restday. Walking cures most things. | Poor back, more like. The rest of me's fine.

scene a_traveller_looking
when topic=player
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : See that traveller? Been looking at my apples three times now. Hungry, I'd say. | That one's not from here. They look at the square like it might bite. | A traveller with a full pack and an empty face. Wants feeding, I'd guess.
b : Give them one. | It might. | Feed them, then.
a : I will, if they ask. Pride's a thing; I'd not trample it. | Only if they haggle. Otherwise we're a friendly square. | Soup's on at home. Greens I can spare. One or the other.

story the_first_trestle [topic=work|memories self.age>=30 self.role=merchant]
  : My first trestle was a door off its hinges on two crates, and I sold windfall apples off it.
  : The first day I sold four. Four apples. I went home and told everybody like it was a fortune.
  : The second day it rained and the door warped, and I sold the apples off my lap.
  ask : Can you remember the first thing you sold?
  b : Nothing so honest as apples.
  : Then you missed a good lesson. Four apples were my first master, and a good one.

story the_frost_in_may [topic=season|memories self.age>=30 self.role=merchant]
  : One year a frost came after the blossom. One night, and every farm I bought from was black by morning.
  : No lettuce, no beans, nothing green for a month. I sold nettles, and I'm not ashamed.
  : Folk bought them. Made soup of them. We all got through it.
  ask : Have you ever eaten nettle soup?
  b[old] : In hard years. More than once.
  b : Never. Doesn't it sting?
  : Not once it's boiled. Like most troubles, really. You boil them down and they're food.

story the_carrot_thief [topic=memories|animals self.age>=25]
  : Back when my fur was all one colour, a goat lived on the square and belonged to nobody.
  : It ate from every stall and nobody could catch it. The Watch tried. Twice.
  : In the end an old stallholder set carrots in a row out through the gate, and the goat ate its way out of the city and never came back.
  ask : Chase it, or carrots? Which for you?
  b : Carrots. I'm not daft.
  : Then you'd have done better than the Watch, and they had spears.

story the_long_walk_north [topic=pastimes|memories self.age>=25]
  : Once I went on foot to the farms, to see where the greens came from. Took me all day.
  : Rows and rows of cabbages, and the farmer stared as if I'd dropped from the moon.
  : I stood in a field and ate a carrot out of the ground. Best thing I ever tasted.
  b : Straight from the soil? Did the farmer mind?
  : Charged me a farthing for it. Fair. I'd have charged two.

story the_wet_floor_fall [topic=memories|weather self.age>=25]
  : I came down on wet boards once with a crate of eggs. Not mine; I was minding the stall next door.
  : Thirty eggs. Not one survived. I sat in the mess and laughed till the egg-seller came back.
  : I paid for every one, and I've hated wet floors ever since.
  ask : Have you ever laughed at the worst moment?
  b : At a burying, once. I've never forgotten it.
  : Some things are too bad not to laugh at. Eggs are one of them.

rule @greens_cry
when concept=bark working=yes self.role=merchant
does aside
cooldown 6h
say Greens! Dawn-fresh, and they'll tell you so themselves! | Leeks, lettuce, turnips! Squeeze them; they don't mind!

rule @dusk_cheap
when concept=bark working=yes self.role=merchant here.time=dusk
does aside
cooldown 10h
say Last of the day, half price, and still better than a stale loaf! | Going cheap! Tired greens, honest price!

rule @warm_rain_bark
when concept=bark here.weather=rain here.season=summer|spring
does aside
cooldown 9h
say There's the rain. Drink up, lettuces. | Warm as a bath. Best weather there is.

rule @wet_boards_bark
when concept=bark here.weather=rain here.season=autumn|winter
does aside
cooldown 10h
say Boards like a pond. Careful, all. | Who brought the gutter in under my awning?

rule @carts_late_bark
when concept=bark world.wagons=late self.role=merchant
does aside
cooldown 10h
say Nothing on the road yet. I'll sell the crates next. | Empty north road, empty trestle.

rule @pigeon_bark
when concept=bark self.role=merchant here.time=day
does aside
cooldown 12h
say Off, you! That's not your lettuce! | The white-winged one's back. Watch the crates.

dlg @1 resident.hub : #greet#. Something green, or just passing the time? | #greet#. Ask away; the cabbages won't listen.
dlg @2 resident.hub [topic.meeting=known] : #topic.player#! I kept a good one back, in case. | #greet#, #topic.player#. Still not eating your greens, I see.
dlg @3 resident.hub [here.time=night] : Late, and I'm tired. Short questions, short answers. | #greet#. I'm off home to my soup. Quick, then.
dlg @4 resident.hub [pair.disposition=hostile|wary] : That's a sour look. Even my lemons don't look that sour. | Speak from where you stand. The trestle's between us for a reason.
dlg @1 resident.job [self.role=merchant self.job=merchant] : Greens on Market Square. I'm at the gate before light for first pick, and here till the last leek's gone. | #self.trade_words.capitalize#. Cabbages since I could lift one, and I've not tired of them.
dlg @2 resident.job [self.working=yes] : You're looking at it. Stack, sell, stack again. | This, all day. Mind the radishes.
dlg @3 resident.job : Work, walks, and a reckoning of who grows the best food. That's me, more or less. | Honest work, and soup waiting when it's done.
dlg @1 resident.news [world.news] : A driver from #world.news_from# had this: #world.news#. | The drivers say #world.news#. They had it in #world.news_from#.
dlg @2 resident.news [world.event_words] : Everybody at the trestle's on about #world.event_words#. | Every stall's on about #world.event_words#, top of the square to bottom.
dlg @3 resident.news [world.wagons=raided] : Somebody's wagon was robbed up the road. I'm short of greens because of it. | Wagons robbed up north again. Keep within the walls, I would.
dlg @4 resident.news [world.death=recent] : A funeral lately. The square went quiet for a whole morning. | We lost one of ours lately. I sent soup round. Soup's my way of saying it.
dlg @5 resident.news [world.wedding=recent] : A wedding! I sold more greens for the feast than in a fortnight. | Somebody's married, and the square's still smiling about it.
dlg @6 resident.news [!world.news !world.event_words world.wagons!=raided world.death!=recent world.wedding!=recent] : #news_none# The carts brought cabbages and nothing else. | Quiet. The biggest news on my trestle is a carrot shaped like a hand.
dlg @1 resident.gossip [here.gossip=yes] : Off the trestle this morning: #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. Word for word, as I had it. | They say #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. I'm only the greengrocer; don't quote me.
dlg @2 resident.gossip [here.gossip=yes topic.claim_kind=theft|wanted] : Count your coins. They say #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. | They're saying #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. My till's sleeping in my coat.
dlg @3 resident.gossip [here.gossip!=yes] : Nothing worth repeating. The cabbages are discreet. | #gossip_none# Try again on Marketday.
dlg @1 resident.business [business=thriving] : Selling out by noon. I've had to buy a second crate of everything. | Couldn't be better. The jar under the floor's getting heavy.
dlg @2 resident.business [business=steady] : Steady. Folk need their greens, and I've always got them. | Steady as a turnip. No complaints.
dlg @3 resident.business [business=failing] : Poorly, and no use hiding it. Folk aren't buying, and the greens won't wait. | Bad. I'm selling what I can and giving away the rest.
dlg @1 resident.bye : #farewell#. Eat something green. | #farewell#. Tell them the greens are honest here.
dlg @2 resident.bye [here.time=night] : Night. Soup's waiting. | #farewell#. The carts come early.
dlg @1 shop.greet [here.shop!=bare self.role=merchant] : #greet#. Squeeze anything you like; it's all fresh this morning. | #greet#, #address#. Leeks, lettuce, roots. Two for a farthing on the small ones.
dlg @2 shop.greet [here.shop!=bare topic.meeting=known] : #topic.player#. The crisp ones are tucked behind the crate, kept for you. | Back again, #topic.player#. The good farm sent something special.
dlg @3 shop.greet [here.shop=bare] : #greet#. Sold to the last radish. Come at dawn tomorrow. | The trestle's bare. Even the pigeons gave up. Tomorrow, early.
dlg @4 shop.greet [here.shop!=bare] : #greet#. #shop_offer# Everything's from this morning's carts. | #greet#. Have a look. Don't mind the cat; it's resting.
dlg @1 shop.prices : Cheap if it's summer, dear if it's winter, and fair every season. | Prices follow the carts. Late carts, dear greens. That's the whole secret.
dlg @2 shop.prices [world.wagons=late] : Late wagons, dear greens. No sense hiding it. | Up this week. Blame the north road, not me.
dlg @1 shop.bye : #shop_bye# Wash them before you eat them. | #farewell#. Keep them cool and they'll keep.
dlg @1 master.learn [here.teaches=yes self.role=merchant] : Greens? It's up in the dark and wet paws all winter. Still keen? Then I'll teach you to choose. | First lesson: squeeze, smell, look underneath. Second lesson: the same, but faster.
dlg @2 master.learn [here.teaches=yes] : #here.trade_title.capitalize#? Early hours and sore feet. Ask what you like. | It's learned by doing it beside me. Come at dawn and see.
dlg @3 master.learn [here.teaches=no] : One hand's all my trestle can feed, and I've got one. Questions cost nothing. | #self.hand.capitalize# has the learning of it now. Ask me anything else.

graft resident.hub
choice Which farm grows the best greens? -> league
node league
say A league in my head, week by week. No names on the square; farmers have ears in every crate. This week's top farm sent leeks thick as your wrist.
mood pleased
free
choice How do you judge them? -> league_how
choice Thanks. Back to it. -> hub
node league_how
say Tight heads, crisp leaves, no slug holes, and how they look after three days on my trestle. A good farm's greens still stand up straight on day three.
choice I'll squeeze before I buy. -> hub
choice Goodbye. -> bye

graft shop.greet
choice What's freshest today? [self.role=merchant] -> freshest
node freshest
say Whatever's at the front. I put the best in front, always; hiding the good stuff at the back is how a stall loses friends. Today, the lettuce.
mood warm
choice Let me see the greens. -> greet
choice Why the front? -> front
node front
say Because the eye buys before the teeth do. Show them the best and they trust the rest. Show them the worst and they walk.
choice Let me see the greens. -> greet
choice Goodbye. -> bye
